  "worked_solution": "The point $P(1,2,1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector ${r}^{\u2032}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{\u2032}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+2\\,j+k \\\\ t\\,i+({t}^{3}+1)\\,j+{t}^{5}\\,k & =i+2\\,j+k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}+1=2 \\\\ {t}^{5}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satifies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,2,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$  \n\nWe calculate ${r}^{\u2032}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5})\\,k \\\\  & =i+3{t}^{2}\\,j+5{t}^{4}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(1) & =i+3(1)^{2}\\,j+5(1)^{4}\\,k \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+5\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P(1,2,1)$ is \n\n$$\nR(u)=i+2\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$",
